Planning a Research Study

The first step in planning a research study is to develop an answerable research question. A thoughtful research question acknowledges and/or addresses a knowledge gap.1

The Port Discovery Galactic Builders project identifies the problem: Identify the blockers that are preventing older children (ages 8 to 12) from enjoying the Galactic Builders exhibit.

What are we trying to accomplish? Remove blockers that are preventing older children from enjoying the exhibit. 

A research question should consist of information on participants, intervention, comparison, outcomes and study design (PICOS).1

In the above example, using the PICOS approach, a more specific research question can be framed: 

Population: Children in the 8 - 12 age group should be included in the research study.

Intervention: Who will moderate the discussion with the children and their activities during the research study? 

Comparison: Does there need to be a comparison group?

Outcomes: How will the outcome be arrived at? What steps in the research study will help us answer the question and/or solve the problem for Port Discovery. 

Study design: The research team from the University of Baltimore certainly wanted to conduct the study in a participatory design format, with the children designing the Galactic Builders space with adults providing the Bags of Stuff with supplies and prompts, while also asking questions to explain the reasoning behind their designs. 

A research question should be:2

Clear: Specific and detailed enough so that the reader can easily understand the purpose. 
Focused: Narrow enough that it can be answered thoroughly. 
Concise: Brief but comprehensive. 
Novel: A new concept or approach.
Original: The principal investigator should synthesize an original concept with his unique knowledge that is not available in the same form from previous studies.
Knowledge contribution: What is already known about that topic? What is the new knowledge gain with this research question?

Of the features above, our research question fulfilled all the criteria above. 

Our team from the University of Baltimore got perspective of the work, research methods and implementation undertaken at Port Discovery. We learned that Port Discovery does most of their research through direct observation of children at play. Our team was one of the few instances when they used a formal research team to evaluate their problem and provide a solution. 

Who’s Who

The University of Baltimore team of researchers consisted of eight members, who undertook different roles during different parts of the research study. 

During the participatory design sessions, two researchers paired with the child designers (3-4 per team).

Roles

According to Druin, child designers can perform four roles during the research design process: user, tester, informant and design partner.3

The University of Baltimore researchers planned to have the children as design partners, because only children could give us the insight and expertise at what they would like the Port Discovery Galactic Builders exhibit. 

While both researchers would provide the children with Bags of Stuff/supplies to make the prototype of the Galactic Builders exhibit, one researcher would take notes during the design session, and another would facilitate the design by prompting and discussing the child’s reasoning behind their designs.

Notetaking

While the IDIA 742 students took notes during the participatory design session, it was the testers from the university who made notes on the final prototype. 



1 Shivshankar, R., Singh, K., Gupta, P., Primer of Epidemiology V: Planning a research study and sampling methods, 2021, The National Medical Journal of India, 34(5)

2 Yoon, U., Planning a research study, 2021, The Practical Guide to Clinical Research and Publication, 53-67.

3 Druin, A., The role of children in the design of new technology, 2002, Behaviour & Information Technology, 1-25, 21(1)
